  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0077 database is a comprehensive system for managing various entities, including API tokens, delayed jobs, email templates, and user interactions. It contains 18 tables that work together to store and manage data. The api_tokens table stores information about API access tokens, including their names, tokens, and creation and update timestamps. The delayed_jobs table tracks scheduled tasks, such as priority, attempts, and last error messages. Email templates are stored in the email_templates table, which includes the template name, subject, content, and creation and update timestamps. The history_items table keeps a record of historical events, including the item type, item ID, and date. The issue_service_joins table links issues to services, while the issue_updates table tracks updates to issues, including the issue ID, user ID, service status ID, and update timestamp. The issues table stores information about issues, such as title, state, and creation and update timestamps. The maintenance_service_joins table links maintenance events to services, and the maintenance_updates table tracks updates to maintenance events, including the maintenance ID, user ID, and update timestamp. The maintenances table stores information about maintenance events, including title, description, start and finish times, and creation and update timestamps. The nifty_attachments table stores file attachments, including the parent ID, parent type, token, and file name. The schema_migrations table tracks database schema changes, with a single version number as the primary key. The service_groups table stores information about service groups, including the group name and creation and update timestamps. The service_statuses table stores information about service statuses, including the status name, permalink, color, and status type. The services table stores information about services, including the service name, permalink, position, and creation and update timestamps. The sites table stores information about websites, including the title, description, domain, and support email. The subscribers table stores information about subscribers, including their email addresses and verification tokens. Finally, the users table stores information about users, including their email addresses, names, and password digests.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nStaytus is a status page platform developed using Rails. It serves organizations needing to communicate real-time issues and maintenance events to their customers, ensuring transparency for web applications, networks, or services they provide, which are expected to be accessible 24/7. \n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to create a comprehensive solution that allows organizations to publish and manage the latest information about service issues and maintenance activities, ensuring timely communication with users and stakeholders.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n1. Organizations who need to communicate service status to customers.\n2. System Administrators responsible for managing and updating the status information.\n3. End Users who rely on the organization's services and subscribe to updates.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. User Interface\n   - Responsive and attractive design for both public and admin views.\n   - Customizable themes for branding needs.\n\n2. Issue Tracking\n   - Admins can log and update issues as they occur.\n   - Continuous posting of updates on existing issues.\n\n3. Maintenance Management\n   - Admins can schedule and announce maintenance sessions.\n   - Provide real-time updates during maintenance activities.\n\n4. Email Notifications\n   - Users can subscribe to service updates via email.\n   - Automated notifications for new issues and scheduled maintenance.\n\n5. API Integration\n   - JSON API for updating statuses from external systems.\n   - Integration capability with existing monitoring solutions.\n\n6. Collaborative Updates\n   - Multiple users can update the site to keep information current.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Performance\n   - Efficient data handling optimized for near real-time updates.\n\n2. Scalability\n   - Capable of accommodating a growing number of users and updates.\n\n3. Security\n   - Secure access for administrators and sensitive data handling.\n\n4. Reliability\n   - Must ensure uptime to provide continuous access to status information.\n\n5. Usability\n   - Intuitive and user-friendly interfaces across all devices.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0077.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0077\nCREATE TABLE `api_tokens` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `delayed_jobs` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `priority` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `attempts` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_error` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `email_templates` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `template_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `subject` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `history_items` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `item_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `item_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `issue_service_joins` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `issue_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `service_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `issue_service_joins_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`issue_id`) REFERENCES `issues` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `issue_service_joins_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`service_id`) REFERENCES `services` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `issue_updates` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `issue_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `service_status_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `issue_updates_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`issue_id`) REFERENCES `issues` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `issue_updates_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `issue_updates_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`service_status_id`) REFERENCES `service_statuses` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `issues` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `state` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance_service_joins` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `maintenance_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `service_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_service_joins_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`maintenance_id`) REFERENCES `maintenances` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_service_joins_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`service_id`) REFERENCES `services` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenance_updates` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `maintenance_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_updates_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`maintenance_id`) REFERENCES `maintenances` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `maintenance_updates_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `maintenances` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `start_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `finish_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `nifty_attachments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `parent_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `parent_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `file_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `schema_migrations` (\n  `version` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`version`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `service_groups` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `service_statuses` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `status_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permalink` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `color` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `status_type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `services` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `service_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `permalink` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `position` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sites` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text NOT NULL,\n  `domain` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `support_email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `subscribers` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `verification_token` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password_digest` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
